    "new": "do the same job at pH 7.4 without strong acid or base.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Saponification of a fat. Tristearin, a triacylglycerol, is drawn as a glycerol backbone of three stacked carbons, CH2, CH and CH2, each bonded through an oxygen to an ester carbonyl group, C double-bond O, that carries a (CH2)16CH3 chain. Tristearin plus 3 NaOH, heated, gives glycerol, CH2OH–CHOH–CH2OH, plus 3 sodium stearate, CH3(CH2)16COO minus Na plus, which is soap.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k2sap}}/fig-p597116-saponification-tristearin.svg\" fileid=\"{{k2sap}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 6.8.2: Saponification of a fat. Heating tristearin, a triacylglycerol (three ester groups on one glycerol backbone), with NaOH splits all three esters, giving glycerol and three sodium stearate units, which are soap. One NaOH is used up for each ester. (Structures drawn by Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
