    "old": "so the reaction is called <em>regioselective</em>.</p>",
    "new": "so the reaction is called <em>regioselective</em>.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Markovnikov addition of HCl to propene. Propene, CH2=CH–CH3, in which the end CH2 carbon of the double bond has 2 H and the middle CH carbon has 1 H, plus hydrogen chloride, H–Cl, gives 2-chloropropane as the major product: the H (red) adds to the CH2 carbon, which already had more hydrogens, making it CH3, and the Cl (red) adds to the middle carbon, giving CH3–CHCl–CH3. A note says the minor product is 1-chloropropane, CH3CH2CH2Cl.\" width=\"560\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k2mark}}/fig-p597081-propene-hcl.svg\" fileid=\"{{k2mark}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 3.4.1: Markovnikov&#39;s rule for HCl and propene. The H (red) adds to the end CH<sub>2</sub> carbon, which already has more hydrogens, and the Cl adds to the middle carbon, so 2-chloropropane is the major product; 1-chloropropane forms only as a minor product. (Structures drawn by Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
