    "new": "Every O and N belongs to exactly one functional group. &#10003;</p>\n</div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Skeletal structure of aspartame with each functional group circled and labeled in color: the amine, NH2 (blue), at the left; the carboxylic acid, C=O with OH (red), on the side chain below it; the amide, C=O bonded to NH (orange), joining the two halves of the molecule; the ester, C=O bonded to O–CH3 (purple), at the right; and the aromatic ring, a benzene ring (green), at the top right.\" width=\"374\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k2asp}}/fig-p597059-aspartame-groups.svg\" fileid=\"{{k2asp}}\" /><figcaption>Figure 1.6.7: The same carbonyl-first hunt on a bigger molecule, the sweetener aspartame. It has five functional groups: amine, carboxylic acid, amide, ester and aromatic ring. The N of the amide and the O&ndash;CH<sub>3</sub> oxygen of the ester belong to those groups and are not counted again as an amine or an ether. (Structures drawn by Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
