  "597118": [
   {
    "old": "which is the form that crosses membranes.</p>",
    "new": "which is the form that crosses membranes.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Aspirin in two places in the body. Left panel, labeled stomach (strong acid): –COOH keeps its H. Skeletal aspirin, a benzene ring carrying an acetate ester group, O–C(=O)–CH3, and next to it a carboxylic acid group, C(=O)OH, which is boxed in blue; note under it: neutral, crosses membranes. Right panel, labeled blood (pH 7.4): –COO minus, lost its H. The same structure with the acid group drawn as the carboxylate ion, C(=O)O minus, boxed in red; note under it: charged, stays in water.\" width=\"660\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k2asa}}/fig-p597118-aspirin-stomach-blood.svg\" fileid=\"{{k2asa}}\" /><figcaption>Figure \\(\\PageIndex{1}\\): Aspirin in the stomach and in the blood. In strong stomach acid the &ndash;COOH group keeps its H, so the molecule is neutral and can cross cell membranes; at blood pH 7.4 it has lost its H, and the charged &ndash;COO<sup>&minus;</sup> form stays dissolved in water. (Structures drawn by Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
    "count": 1,
    "note": "6.10 Ionized or not at body pH: aspirin COOH vs COO-"
   }
