  "597111": [
   {
    "old": "dispersion forces grow with the chain.</p>",
    "new": "dispersion forces grow with the chain.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Two side-by-side comparisons of hydrogen bonding. Left: two acetic acid molecules, CH3COOH, face each other head to head. The O-H hydrogen of each acid is joined by a dotted hydrogen bond to the C=O oxygen of the other acid, closing a ring, so the pair is held by two hydrogen bonds. Right: two ethanol molecules, CH3CH2OH; the O-H hydrogen of one is joined by a single dotted hydrogen bond to the oxygen of the other, so the pair is held by one hydrogen bond. Lone pairs are not shown.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k1dimer}}/fig-p597111-acid-dimer.svg\" fileid=\"{{k1dimer}}\" /><figcaption>Figure \\(\\PageIndex{2}\\): Two acetic acid molecules line up head-to-head and hold each other with <strong>two</strong> hydrogen bonds, forming a dimer; a pair of ethanol molecules is held by only one. That is why carboxylic acids boil higher than alcohols of similar size. (Diagram: Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
    "count": 1,
    "note": "insert Figure \\(\\PageIndex{2}\\) (fig-p597111-acid-dimer.svg)"
   }
  ],
