    "old": "so the molecule should be CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.</li>\n</ul>",
    "new": "so the molecule should be CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Four Lewis structures that students drew, each in its own box. (a) Methanol, CH3OH, as drawn: carbon bonded to three H and to O, and the O bonded to one H with three lone pairs. Corrected: the O has two lone pairs. Error: too many electrons; the drawing uses 16 electrons but methanol has 14. (b) Formaldehyde, H2C=O: carbon bonded to two H and double-bonded to O, which has two lone pairs. This one is correct: 12 electrons, carbon has four bonds, oxygen has two bonds and two lone pairs. (c) Ethanethiol, CH3CH2SH, as drawn: the sulfur has two bonds and only one lone pair. Corrected: the sulfur has two lone pairs. Error: octet not satisfied; S has only 6 electrons. (d) Methylamine, as drawn: the nitrogen is bonded to the carbon and to three H, with no lone pair. Corrected: CH3NH2, nitrogen bonded to carbon and two H with one lone pair. Error: four bonds and no lone pair is the pattern of a positive nitrogen, as in CH3NH3+.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/{{k1lewis}}/fig-p597056-lewis-errors.svg\" fileid=\"{{k1lewis}}\" /><figcaption>Figure \\(\\PageIndex{1}\\): The four structures of Example 1 drawn out, each beside its corrected form. (a) too many electrons on O; (b) correct; (c) S without an octet; (d) N with four bonds and no lone pair, which is the pattern of N<sup>+</sup>. (Diagram: Daniel Kwasniewski, CC BY 4.0.)</figcaption></figure>",
